-- How a software release becomes announceable - the checklist as law.
--
-- Every project has release rules: don't tag a commit the test gate
-- never blessed, don't announce before every platform's download
-- exists, never reuse a version number. Usually they live in a
-- checklist and in people's heads, and the day someone is tired or
-- hurried, a step gets skipped and nobody notices until a user does.
--
-- This programme turns that checklist into rules a runtime enforces.
-- A release that skipped a step is not "risky" here - the change that
-- would skip it simply refuses to save. The claims are admitted by a
-- person running one command per step during a real release; nothing
-- is automated, and that is the point of the first stage: the law is
-- worth having before the plumbing is.
--
-- It is also this project governing itself. Each rule below has a
-- near-miss in our own history; writing them as law is the same move
-- we ask of every domain we model.

program release_governance

-- The vocabulary. A platform in the release matrix; a commit the full
-- test gate ran green at; a version tagged at a commit; a built
-- download for a version on a platform; the changelog entry; the
-- public announcement.
--
-- `append only` says history is never edited: a gate run, a tag, an
-- announcement can never be quietly retracted - a mistake is fixed by
-- a new version, never by rewriting what happened. The tag also
-- carries `unique by`: one commit per version, forever, so "which
-- code was v0.0.8?" always has exactly one answer.
predicate PlatformDeclared(platform: Subject)
predicate GateGreen(version: Subject, commit: Subject)
    append only
predicate Tagged(version: Subject, commit: Subject)
    unique by (version)
    append only
predicate AssetPublished(version: Subject, platform: Subject)
predicate ChangelogEntry(version: Subject)
predicate Announced(version: Subject)
    append only

intent ReleaseAnnounced(version: Subject)

-- The rules that must always hold, whatever anyone does.

-- A tag exists only at a commit whose gate ran green. Both sides are
-- append-only, so this is safe as an always-rule: no later change can
-- pull the gate run out from under an existing tag.
invariant tagged_commit_ran_the_gate:
    Tagged(version, commit) implies GateGreen(version, commit)

-- An announcement never exists without its tag and its changelog.
invariant announced_release_is_tagged_and_documented:
    Announced(version) implies (Tagged(version, _) and ChangelogEntry(version))

-- The steps of a release, each a single command.

transformation declare_platform(platform):
    admit PlatformDeclared(platform)

transformation record_gate(version, commit):
    admit GateGreen(version, commit)

transformation tag_release(version, commit):
    require gate_ran_green_at_this_commit: GateGreen(version, commit)
    admit Tagged(version, commit)

transformation publish_asset(version, platform):
    require version_is_tagged: Tagged(version, _)
    require platform_is_in_the_matrix: PlatformDeclared(platform)
    admit AssetPublished(version, platform)

transformation record_changelog(version):
    admit ChangelogEntry(version)

-- The act the whole programme exists for. Note that "every declared
-- platform has its download" is a gate on THIS act, deliberately not
-- an always-rule: the matrix can grow next year, and declaring a new
-- platform must not reach back and make last year's complete
-- announcement retroactively unlawful. A gate governs what you may do
-- next; an always-rule governs what is true - and completeness of the
-- matrix is only ever a fact about the moment of announcing.
transformation announce(version):
    require version_is_tagged: Tagged(version, _)
    require changelog_written: ChangelogEntry(version)
    require every_declared_platform_has_its_asset: (forall p in PlatformDeclared(p): AssetPublished(version, p))
    admit Announced(version)
    emit ReleaseAnnounced(version)
